vboxfront (1.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Import a compressed raw image without unpacking it first
    (`convertfromraw stdin`). A 40 GB .img.gz no longer has to be extracted
    to a 40 GB temp file: the archive is decompressed straight into
    VBoxManage's standard input and no unpacked copy is ever written. gzip,
    bzip2 and xz, all from the standard library. The byte count the command
    needs is mandatory and is the uncompressed length — gzip records one
    below 4 GiB and it is filled in automatically; the others are measured by
    one streaming pass over the (smaller) archive, which the dialog does on
    request and shows progress for.
  * Export to a flat raw image (`clonemedium --format RAW`). RAW never
    appears in a format combo, because its backend registers no hard-disk
    extension and so can never be a managed medium — but it is a perfectly
    good clone target, and this was the one direction the app could not go.
    The result is what feeds dd, a USB stick or qemu.
  * Media health can now clean up after itself: one guided pass unregisters
    every entry whose backing file is gone, which is the commonest way a
    registry rots. Plain closemedium, never --delete — there is no file left
    to delete.
  * Media health reports disks attached without --discard, which is why
    compacting them so often reclaims nothing: without it the guest's TRIM
    never reaches the image. The attach dialog has always offered the flag
    and nothing ever reported its absence.
  * A medium held only by a snapshot is now explained rather than failing
    obscurely. storageattach edits a VM's current state only, so there is no
    slot to clear; Detach used to answer "could not locate the attachment
    slot", which read like a bug in the app.
  * The orphan scan can say why an image is unregistered. Usually its UUID
    collides with one already registered — a copied .vdi — and the fix is
    Image UUID tools, which the app has shipped since 1.2.0 with nothing
    pointing at it.
  * Attach and Properties take a selection, as compact, remove, detach and
    convert have since 1.6.0. Attach fills consecutive free slots, stepping
    over occupied ones rather than letting storageattach replace them
    silently; Properties shows what a selection has in common and sends only
    what was changed — an empty description box never blanks differing ones.
  * A visible queue. A batch of thirty compacts was one number in the status
    bar; it now lists what is still waiting, with "Skip the rest", which
    drops the queue and leaves the running command alone.
  * Plan mode (File → Plan mode): every batch shows the exact commands it
    would run, with Copy as script and Save as…, and runs only if you say so
    there. Portable to a machine with no GUI, or into a change ticket.
  * Command history can be saved as a shell script — the selected commands,
    or all of them. A command whose password went over stdin is flagged in
    the file rather than left to fail at three in the morning.
  * A registry snapshot is written automatically before a batch remove, a
    batch convert or a health cleanup. It cannot undo anything — the trash
    does that for files — but it records every UUID and location as they were.
  * A details pane (Media → Details pane, Ctrl+J) for the selected medium.
    Everything in it is already in the listing on screen, so opening it runs
    nothing; Info shells out and prints into the output panel, which had made
    the panel a viewer.
  * A structured filter: `fmt:vdi`, `size:>10G`, `capacity:<2T`, `vm:alpha`,
    `state:inaccessible` and the rest, alongside the plain substring match.
    A term that names no field — a pasted Windows path, say — stays a plain
    word.
  * Elapsed time and an estimate on the progress bar, and a window alert plus
    a one-line verdict when something that ran over thirty seconds finishes.
    Nothing is guessed below 5% or in the first seconds, where the number
    would swing more than it would help.
  * The registry is fingerprinted on every refresh, and a change that no
    command of ours explains is reported. The VirtualBox GUI, a VM starting
    or a second copy of this app can all edit it, and a stale row is how an
    action lands on the wrong medium.
  * Refreshes are much cheaper on a large library: a library entry whose file
    has not changed since it was last opened is no longer re-opened, which
    was one process per entry on every F5. An entry the listing stops showing
    — what a VBoxSVC restart looks like — is always opened again.
  * A default folder for new images, in Settings, falling back to
    VirtualBox's own default machine folder instead of the home directory.
    Each dialog then remembers the folder it was last used in.
  * Colours come from the palette rather than being hardcoded, so the greys
    and the inline warnings stay readable on a dark theme.
  * A column chooser on the list header (eleven columns is a horizontal
    scroll on a laptop), and shortcuts for compact, resize, convert, attach,
    detach, properties, health, history and undo delete.
  * Resizing a disk with differencing children is refused with the child
    named, the guard Format as FAT already had.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Deleting a disk image is now undoable. Remove's delete action no longer
    passes --delete to closemedium, which unlinks the file for good: the
    medium is unregistered and VBoxFront moves the backing file into a trash
    folder itself — by default a hidden .vboxfront-trash/ beside the image,
    so the move is a rename inside one filesystem and costs nothing however
    large the disk is. Media → Undo delete puts the last delete back for the
    rest of the session, a batch remove as one batch, library entry included.
    The trash folder can be moved, or the whole thing turned off (back to a
    real --delete), in File → Settings. Nothing is ever emptied
    automatically, a name already taken in the trash is suffixed rather than
    overwritten, and an undo refuses to write over a file that has reappeared
    at the original path.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a medium being dropped from the listing and replaced by a phantom
    row. VBoxManage prints description continuations flush left, so a
    description holding a blank line followed by a "UUID:" line looked
    exactly like the start of the next record: the real medium never reached
    its Location and was discarded, while the phantom that took its place
    carried a UUID with a newline embedded in it — which then went into
    whatever command was run on that row. A "UUID:" inside a description now
    only opens a record once the one being read has a Location, which is the
    point at which VBoxManage could have finished printing it.
  * Fix media vanishing from the tree when a Parent UUID loops. An image
    whose parent is itself — which `sethdparentuuid` will happily stamp — or
    a pair that name each other were both left with no root to hang from, so
    nothing drew them while the totals line went on counting them. An image
    that is not on screen cannot be selected, inspected or repaired. Every
    medium now gets a row whatever the chain says.
  * Fix a batch convert destroying a disk it was about to convert. The
    existing targets are all deleted before the queue starts (clonemedium
    refuses a target that is already there), so a plan that aimed one disk's
    clone at another's backing file destroyed that disk before its own turn
    came round. Re-running a convert over a folder that already held its
    output did exactly that, with the default suffix. Refused outright now.
  * Convert and batch convert name the registered medium a target belongs to
    before overwriting it. Saying yes to "Overwrite?" also deletes whatever
    VirtualBox has registered at that path, leaving a registry entry pointing
    at a file that is no longer the medium it names; that is now a decision
    rather than a surprise.
  * `clonemedium --existing` and `mediumio cat --output=` now count as
    destructive when re-run from the history, so they need the word RUN typed
    like any other command that overwrites something. `--existing` is the one
    clone mode that writes into an image that is already there, and mediumio
    overwrites its output file without asking.
  * Resize says why a disk with differencing children cannot grow, naming the
    child, instead of passing VirtualBox's read-only error along — the same
    guard Format as FAT already had.
  * Exporting to a name ending in .csv or .json writes that format, whatever
    the filter combo happened to be showing. Picking the JSON filter and
    typing "report.csv" used to write JSON into a .csv file.
  * Quitting while a command is running now removes its password files.
    QProcess.finished is what normally triggers the shred and it never
    arrives when the process is killed on the way out.
  * New hermetic end-to-end suite (`make e2e`): the real application driven
    against a stateful fake VBoxManage inside a container — a registry that
    persists, real image files, real process launches — so a workflow can be
    checked by what it changed rather than by what it printed. 40 scenarios,
    no VirtualBox, no display and no network. Also runs in CI.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Multi-selection. The media list now takes more than one row, and Compact,
    Remove, Detach and Convert all act on the selection. Batches run one
    command after another and stop at the first failure, as Compact all
    already did.
  * Batch convert: clone several disks into one folder in a single queued run,
    with the target names previewed before anything starts.
  * Format properties (`mediumproperty`): view and edit a medium's
    format-specific properties. Values come from the listing that is already
    on screen and the names from the format's own schema, so nothing extra is
    run to open the dialog. Note that some properties are only read when the
    image is created — VDI's AllocationBlockSize among them: the command
    succeeds and the value stays as it was.
  * Media health: everything wrong with the registry that can be seen without
    running a command — media VirtualBox cannot open, backing files that have
    gone missing while the registry still says they are fine, differencing
    images whose parent is not registered — plus an optional read-only
    `repairhd -dry-run` pass over the VDIs.
  * Scan for unregistered images: walk chosen folders for image files that
    neither VirtualBox nor the library knows about, and add the ones you pick.
  * Export the listing to CSV or JSON, and copy selected rows as TSV. Sizes go
    out as plain megabyte numbers as well as formatted cells, so they can be
    added up somewhere else.
  * Snapshot column: which snapshot an attachment belongs to. The name was
    already in the listing and had never been shown.
  * Drag and drop image files onto the window to add them to the library; a
    dropped raw image offers Import RAW instead.
  * Free-space preflight before create, resize and clone: the target
    filesystem's free space against what the operation will actually take,
    live in the dialog and once more as a confirmation. VBoxManage does not
    reserve space up front, so a target that does not fit fails part-way and
    leaves a partial file behind.
  * Resize: MB/GB unit selector, a current → new delta, an exact-size mode
    (`--resizebyte`, which takes hex and K/M/G suffixes), and a refusal with a
    reason rather than a VirtualBox error when asked to shrink.
  * Convert: choose the target variant (fixed, split) as well as the format,
    and clone into a pre-created image with `--existing`.
  * Attach: the device number now comes from the controller type, so only IDE
    and floppy controllers offer a second device. DVD and floppy attachments
    can point at an empty drive, the Guest Additions ISO or a host drive, with
    `--passthrough`, `--tempeject` and `--forceunmount`.
  * Compact now reports what it actually reclaimed, measured across the
    refresh that follows it.
  * Re-run a command from the history. Refused outright when its password went
    over stdin or through a temp file that no longer exists; destructive
    commands need the word RUN typed, not a default button.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Remember the window between sessions: size and position (including
    maximised state and which screen), the output-panel splitter, the selected
    tab, and each tab's column widths and order. Stored in the same per-user
    settings as the rest of the configuration. Columns are now resizable and
    movable; they are auto-fitted once on a first run.
  * Ask before quitting while a command is still running, rather than killing
    it and possibly leaving a half-written image.
  * Warn when the VBoxManage location set in Settings is not an executable
    file. It was honoured but never checked, so every command failed with a
    bare "Failed to start" and nothing pointed back at Settings.
  * Record the right command in the history when VBoxManage cannot be launched
    at all: the failure was being filed against whichever command ran before
    it, and the one that failed was not recorded.
  * Encryption dialog: "Check current password" button (`checkmediumpwd`).
  * Confirm before hex-dumping more than a megabyte into the output panel.
  * A long command line no longer widens the output panel's header if it is
    set directly rather than through the status helper.
  * Creating a floppy image no longer adds a redundant media-library entry;
    createmedium already registers what it creates.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix `Access Error:` being folded into the State value. VBoxManage prints
    that field, and its unindented "VD: error ..." continuation, for a medium
    whose file it cannot open; because the field was missing from the known
    key list it landed in State, which showed a three-line blob in the State
    column, stopped inaccessible media from being highlighted red, and let
    "Compact all VDIs" pick up a broken disk and abort the batch on it. The
    reason is now its own field, shown in the State tooltip and in the Resolve
    dialog.
  * Remove: refuse a medium that has differencing children, instead of letting
    closemedium fail with VBOX_E_OBJECT_IN_USE.
  * Keep a long command line from dragging the output panel's header past the
    window edge; it is elided, with the full line in the tooltip.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Contents: read a byte range straight out of an image without mounting it
    (`mediumio cat`) — hex dump into the output panel or extract the range to
    a file. Works on encrypted media, which refuse to be read otherwise.
  * Create differencing images (`createmedium --diffparent`): the listing
    already drew these chains as a tree, now it can build one.
  * Format a medium as FAT (`mediumio formatfat`), and create floppy images
    pre-formatted (`--variant Formatted`) so they are usable straight away.
  * Ask VirtualBox which formats exist instead of hardcoding them
    (`list hddbackends`): QCOW, QED and Parallels are now offered where they
    apply, each with only the variants its backend supports, and read-only
    formats are correctly never offered as targets.
  * Immutable disks: expose `--autoreset`, and say plainly that VirtualBox
    will not change the type of a differencing image rather than surfacing
    that as a COM error.
  * Attach: optional `--mtype`, `--discard` (guest TRIM, which is what makes
    compaction pay off), `--nonrotational` and `--hotpluggable`.
  * Refuse to format a medium that has differencing children — a parent is
    read-only and VirtualBox rejects the write.
  * Deadline read-only VBoxManage calls, so a wedged VirtualBox service can
    no longer strand the refresh chain and silently stop the window updating.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Resolve: rescue a medium VirtualBox can no longer read — re-point the
    registry at a file that moved (`modifymedium --setlocation`), or check
    and repair the image itself (`internalcommands repairhd`, dry run first).
  * Image UUID tools: stamp a fresh UUID into a copied image so it can be
    registered alongside the original, or reattach a differencing image to
    its parent (`internalcommands sethduuid`/`sethdparentuuid`).
  * Change an encryption password in one step, without decrypting first.
  * Show the cipher and password ID of encrypted media in a new Encryption
    column, and pre-select the medium's own cipher when re-keying.
  * Per-tab totals: how much capacity is provisioned versus allocated.
  * Command history with exit codes (File → Command history…), plus a
    "Copy command" button on the output panel.
  * Close the child's stdin once the payload is written, so a command that
    reads it can no longer hang the panel indefinitely.

 -- Vlad Ananyev <vlananyev@gmail.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

vboxfront (1.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix disk encryption, which never worked: the "-" password placeholder
    makes VBoxManage prompt on a terminal, so it failed on a pipe before
    reading anything. Passwords now go through the "stdin" placeholder.
  * Fix `list -l` parsing of multi-line descriptions, whose continuation
    lines VBoxManage prints flush left: a description line shaped like
    "UUID: ..." became the record's UUID and retargeted every later
    operation at another medium, and a description containing a blank line
    dropped the medium from the listing entirely.
  * Convert and Import RAW no longer delete the target while the dialog is
    still open, and refuse a target that is the source image itself.
  * Warn before attaching to an occupied controller slot, which
    storageattach silently replaces.
  * Attach uses the medium type of the tab it was started from.
  * Report the version in the window title and via --version.

